{
  "schema_version": "dakp.ner.gold.v1",
  "annotation_policy": "Gold spans are the maximal condition-mention spans a contraindication or indication targets. Every mention uses the single label 'DiseaseOrPhenotypicFeature': named disorders/syndromes and clinical findings/symptoms/condition-states alike (hypersensitivity, pregnancy, bleeding, seizures, elevations, pain, nausea, vomiting, fatigue). The disease-vs-phenotype boundary is deliberately NOT part of the annotation task. 'Maximal' covers qualifiers that change the concept -- anatomical/etiological ('pulmonary hypertension', 'portal hypertension'), severity ('severe heart failure'), and course ('active liver disease') -- because the qualified term denotes a different condition than its head. It does NOT cover temporal or evidential hedges ('recent', 'a history of', 'known', 'suspected'), which say something about the patient record rather than about the condition: gold for 'a recent myocardial infarction' is 'myocardial infarction'. Population descriptors (e.g. 'women of childbearing potential') are subject populations, NOT condition mentions, and get no span. Each surface occurs exactly once in its text so offsets resolve unambiguously.",
